  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers and thunderstorms this afternoon followed by a strong \ncold front bringing a chance of severe weather Tuesday. \n\nTemperatures continue to increase today with highs in the upper 70s \nto upper 80s across the forecast area. Southerly flow increases \nhumidity as well with a warm and moist air mass yielding increasing \ninstability this afternoon. Showers and thunderstorms are \npossible later this afternoon and evening, although coverage \nwill be limited due to shortwave ridging aloft.\n\nShowers and perhaps a thunderstorm continue into Tuesday \nmorning although recent guidance has chances for AM \nprecipitation highest across the northern portions of the \nforecast area. Therefore, SPC has shifted the slight risk for \nsevere weather southeast slightly. In the afternoon, \nthunderstorm chances increase ahead of a potent cold front. \nDespite morning precipitation, guidance shows plenty of \ninstability across the forecast area which combined with \nstrengthening shear leads to an increasing severe weather \nthreat. Damaging wind gusts continue to be the primary hazard \ntype expected although cooling mid level temperatures yields a \nlarge hail threat, mainly across the southern portions of the \nforecast area. Additionally, the potent area of surface low \npressure associated with cold front tracks across the forecast \narea. Where the low pressure system tracks during convective \nactivity will yield a brief tornado threat. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Drier conditions anticipated later this week, with increasing \nprecipitation chances going into the weekend.\n\nBy Thursday, the Great Lakes low will be well off the coast of New \nEngland as high pressure builds overhead here in the Mid-Atlantic, \nbringing reduced precipitation chances and calmer conditions \noverall. Temperatures will start to gradually increase to the upper \n80s during this period, but drier air aloft will limit heat indices \nincreasing beyond the actual air temperature. \n\nMeanwhile, a broad upper-level trough will begin to form over the \nOhio River Valley over the weekend. As this trough tracks east, \ncloud cover & precipitation chances will begin to increase, \nparticularly between Saturday evening and Sunday as the low tracks \noffshore and towards the northeast. Severe probabilities look \nmarginal as of right now, with NCAR's AI ML algorithm only showing a \nlow-end 5%-15% probability as of the 00z runs. Nonetheless, weekend \nshowers are a potential likelihood with this system should the \nsynoptic setup continue to trend toward development over the \nMidwest.",
